// Hello, greyscale: the smallest program that does something to an image.
// Read a colour PNG, take its luminance, write the result back out.
//
// WHAT TO NOTICE. Every line below is a DEFINITION, not a step. `grey` is what
// the luminance of `colour` IS; nothing is computed until one of the goals at
// the bottom asks for a value, and then the engine computes only what that
// value needs. Delete the `save` goal and no file is written -- the rest still
// prints.

import "simpleitk"
import "vox1"

// The input ships beside this program: three colour bars, 240x180, 8-bit RGB.
// Point this at any image SimpleITK can read -- PNG, JPEG, NIfTI, DICOM.
input_path = "doc/gallery/programs/hello/hello-input.png"

colour = ReadImage(input_path)

// ITU-R BT.709 luminance: 0.2126 red + 0.7152 green + 0.0722 blue, the weights
// that match what the eye does with each channel rather than averaging them.
// A single-channel image passes straight through, so this is also a no-op on an
// image that is already grey.
grey = intensity(colour)

// PNG stores integers. The input is 8-bit and `intensity` keeps that range, so
// a cast is all this needs: rescaling instead would stretch the contrast and
// hand back a different image.
grey_png = Cast(grey, GetPixelIDValueFromString("sitkUInt8"))

// min, max, mean, sigma, variance, sum, count -- in that order.
stats = Statistics(grey)

print "min"  index(stats, 0)
print "max"  index(stats, 1)
print "mean" index(stats, 2)

// `save` is a goal like `print`: it asks for a value and writes it where it
// says. The path is a literal; when it has to be computed, call the writer as
// an ordinary expression instead -- `WriteImage(grey_png, <path>)`.
save "output/hello-greyscale/hello-greyscale.png" grey_png
